hey what's up youtube this is dr john  deloney with ramsay network  so i got a special video today and it's  a special request from a family member  they asked me to talk about the  psychology of  road rage not that they struggle with it  or anything  so let's set the stage you are in the  car and you are driving  you're going 75 miles down the highway  you're going five over the speed limit  because you're  one of these noble folks that only  cheats a little bit or  you're dropping off your little kid at  school you're waiting in that god-awful  soulless line of people waiting to  drop little timmy or little susie or a  little whoever off  in front of the school right and  somebody cuts you off and you  instantly wave at them with all but one  finger right you give them you give them  the bird  or you lay on the horn forever one of  those extended  horn honkers so annoying just  or you're in your little car or your  f-150 you think you're all  tough and somebody cut you off and you  zoom up behind them and you get this  close to them and you just follow them  around because you think that makes you  feel tough  in hardcore and i'll show them it's so  lame dude if they hit the brakes the  only person getting hurt in that deal is  your idiotic self but whatever or  somebody cuts you off  they cut in front of you right and you  grip your wheels  you clench your teeth how many of you  have kids that have learned  new words simply by being in the car  with you  while you drive or if you're in texas or  tennessee  and you're driving and somebody cut you  off and you just start thinking  i'm gonna get my gun right i'm a car  carrying whatever like we're not gonna  take it  anymore like seriously right so here's  the deal  if you're driving and somebody cuts you  off if you're going down the highway and  someone blows by at 100 miles  an hour if you're waiting in line at  sonic and somebody  scoots right in front of you and gets  into your parking spot you are suddenly  faced  with two cosmic truths  the first truth is that you are not the  center of the universe  and the second truth and this one hurts  you are in control  of basically nothing  when someone flies by you they cut you  off they cut in line  you feel small you feel lame you feel  out of control powerless you think  hey we have order around here and  there's rules and i was  next and i'm following all of the rules  right  you just feel small you feel slighted  you feel  less than you feel like hey there's a  set of rules and if we all played by the  same rules  then i would feel good about myself and  in these little moments at  75 miles an hour at 100 miles an hour or  three miles an hour in the chick-fil-a  parking lot  the cosmos rears its ugly head and you  realize that you have outsourced your  personal  spiritual and emotional validation this  idea of worthiness and dignity  to how other people think about you  you have outsourced the way you feel to  other people's behavior and this is  totally disorienting when you experience  it  it can unwind you instantly  and you realize that your human default  setting is not set to look for ways  people are  supporting you and caring for you and  putting good out in the world  you realize that you are actively  scanning your environment  all the time for yet another way you're  getting screwed  right and if you spend your time looking  for  places where people aren't being nice  where people aren't treating you the way  you think you should be treated where  people are  screwing you over you will always always  see it you'll always find it right and  so to remedy this disorientation  what do we do we get into their heads  and we decide  from the throne of our cars why they  just did what they did  psychologists call this fundamental  attribution error  that's us playing god that's us getting  inside somebody else's head  and deciding why they are driving so  fast deciding  why they just cut in line in front of us  at the child drop-off line or in the  fast food restaurant  and on the road it usually goes  something like this  i'm driving 75 miles an hour which is  just a little bit against the law  just five miles an hour over because i'm  a good person  i'm a moral person i like to help others  and i'm just better than most other  people and when somebody blows by you at  100 miles an hour  you get into their head and you think  you're driving fast  because you're evil and you suck and  you're a box of goat turds  you're the worst right you're deciding  while they're speeding  in this fundamental attribution era this  spending time in  other people's head is such a  waste of energy it's a waste of time  it's a waste of your soul it's  stupid and on its face it doesn't solve  anything  this remedy this blaming somebody else  this  acknowledging why somebody else is doing  something you're just deciding for them  it doesn't solve anything and then you  get to the truth  and the truth is no matter how much you  work out  no matter how many cool tough guy movies  you watch  no matter how many degrees you have how  smart you are how  safe you are how big your car is how  little your car is  all of it the truth is  you control very very  little almost nothing in fact  the only two things on planet earth that  you control  are your thoughts and your actions  that's it that's it  and when you're driving and somebody cut  you off  you are faced with that stark reality  instantly  david foster wallace talks about this  poetically  in his famous speech this is water it's  a graduation speech he gave to kenyon  college  if you haven't heard it as soon as this  video is over  google it it's incredible right but in  this speech he  calls out this critical idea that we get  to  choose what we think and i love it he  does a great job of explaining this  and so here's what i want you to do the  next time you're driving and somebody  cuts you off  they get too close to you they're  texting and driving  you can assume that they're a total butt  face  that they're the worst they're an idiot  right you can assume that or  you can choose to  put some positive energy out into the  universe to drop a quick  prayer you can assume man i bet he's  going to pick up his daughter from a  pretty dangerous situation hope he gets  there safely you can choose to think man  he may be on the way to the emergency  room  and he's got a sick kid in the back of  the car i hope they get there okay  you get to pick he's an idiot he's a  jerk he's a scumbag  or he's really got to get to wherever  he's going maybe he's just got rocket  diarrhea  and he doesn't want to get embarrassed  right let's give him that one  or maybe if a driver gets too close to  you and they flip you off  maybe they're coming from a hard work  conversation  they're about to lose their job they're  completely frazzled and they chose you  to take it out on  you get to decide whether you absorb it  and you say  i hope you have a better day man i must  i'm going to throw out a quick prayer  for you  or that you're gonna act like a  five-year-old you're gonna act like a  child if somebody flips you off in  traffic  just choose to let it go like a vapor  what's it actually doing to you you get  to choose to work towards making  empathy your default setting to choose  to thinking the best about people's  intentions and their behaviors  and not carrying around this spiky  poisonous baggage  of always assuming everybody's going  through their day trying to find ways  to make your life worse the reality is  most people aren't even thinking about  you work towards making  empathy your default setting not  victimhood  not frustration not anger you can be  right  or you can just choose to enjoy your  life  you can grip the steering wheel make  your blood pressure go up  grind your teeth down smash the  dashboard  that's kind of like punching yourself in  the face and hoping it breaks their nose  it doesn't change their life at all at  the end of the day road rage is dumb  stupid it's idiotic it solves nothing  it just makes your day worse  so choose joy choose laughter choose to  let it go  choose productive thoughts practice this  over time you will change your default  setting  to one of peace one of letting things  roll off  one of smiling wherever it is you go  choose to send people positive thoughts  choose to pray for somebody who cuts you  off  choose to give somebody the a-okay hope  things are okay  when they go flying by  they probably need the positive thoughts  too this is dr john deloney with ramsay  network  hit the subscribe button if you know of  road ragers or you know of people who  just do this  all day send this video to them  give them a like they need one right and  if you have any other questions if  you've got a special video you want me  to do  email me at ask john ramsey  solutions.com  have a great week be kind and i'll talk  to you soon

